WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.619
Here is a card fee in Java and Kotlin: the amount times the rate in basis points, over ten thousand.

2
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:12.909
javap shows what javac made of the Java one.

3
00:00:12.909 --> 00:00:16.124
Seven instructions, and not one variable name.

4
00:00:16.124 --> 00:00:20.179
It reads like noise, until you see the machine it runs on.

5
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:21.917
Each call gets a frame.

6
00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:24.613
Numbered slots hold the local variables.

7
00:00:24.613 --> 00:00:31.352
The operand stack is a pile: instructions pop their inputs off the top, and push their results back.

8
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:35.751
Call it with 125,000 cents and 290 basis points.

9
00:00:35.751 --> 00:00:40.144
amountMinor is a long, and a long fills two slots.

10
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.661
So which slot does bps get?

11
00:00:42.661 --> 00:00:43.696
Pause on it.

12
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:46.235
Slot 2.

13
00:00:46.235 --> 00:00:49.157
That's why the listing says iload_2.

14
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:50.239
Now run it.

15
00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:55.788
lload_0 copies the long from slot 0 onto the stack: the l is for long.

16
00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:59.989
iload_2 copies the int from slot 2: the i is for int.

17
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.691
Next is a multiply, and lmul only takes two longs.

18
00:01:03.691 --> 00:01:05.856
The top of the stack is an int.

19
00:01:05.856 --> 00:01:07.601
What has to happen first?

20
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.034
i2l, int to long.

21
00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:16.300
It pops the 290 and pushes it back as a long, two slots tall.

22
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.600
lmul pops both longs and pushes the product.

23
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:25.419
ldc2_w pushes the long 10,000 from the constant pool.

24
00:01:25.419 --> 00:01:29.319
ldiv pops two longs and pushes 3,625.

25
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.563
lreturn pops it and returns it: 36 euros and 25 cents.

26
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:39.255
At its fullest, the stack held two longs, four slots.

27
00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:44.252
javap -v prints that as stack=4, and the slots as locals=3.

28
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:52.421
kotlinc's version is almost the same: a public static final method, on a class named StackDemoKt.

29
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:54.530
Only the constant differs.

30
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:58.395
javac loads a ready-made long from the constant pool.

31
00:01:58.395 --> 00:02:04.230
kotlinc writes 10,000 into the instruction with sipush, then widens it with i2l.

32
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.908
Same long on the stack.

33
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:14.268
The rule: every instruction pops typed inputs off the stack and pushes its result, and its first letter names the type.

34
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.015
A quick check.

35
00:02:16.015 --> 00:02:19.134
Add plus 30 at the end of the Java version.

36
00:02:19.134 --> 00:02:21.889
What appears between ldiv and lreturn?

37
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:28.896
ldc2_w pushes the long 30, then ladd adds the two longs.
